            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable Perseverance rover news is:\n\n- **Perseverance has completed a “marathon” on Mars.** NASA’s rover reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on the Red Planet, hitting the milestone on **Sol 1,890** after about **five years and four months** of operations. That is much faster than Opportunity, which took more than 11 years to cover the same distance. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE image also captured the rover and its tracks from orbit shortly before the milestone.【6:0†source】\n\n- **The rover is now studying very ancient terrain near/around Jezero Crater’s rim.** NASA/JPL reported that Perseverance found evidence that a **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock, called the **“Broom Point member,”** was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts more than **3.9 billion years ago**. Scientists say this offers a rare record of early solar-system bombardment that is largely erased on Earth by plate tectonics.【6:4†source】\n\n- **Current exploration context:** Perseverance has moved beyond its original Jezero Crater floor and delta investigations and is operating west of Jezero in terrain the team has nicknamed **“Arbot,”** continuing to examine rocks that may preserve some of Mars’ oldest geologic history.【6:0†source】\n\nIn short: the newest headlines are that Perseverance just set a major driving-distance milestone while also returning science from some of the oldest rocks ever examined by a Mars rover.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/07/260713084858.htm",
                    "start_index": 502,
                    "end_index": 514,
                    "title": "NASA's Perseverance just completed a marathon on Mars"
                  },
